The full Docker story (simple, complete, in order)
I start on my computer where I already have all my application files, like source code, data files, and a Dockerfile. The Dockerfile is just a set of steps that tell Docker how to build something called an image. Nothing is running yet.
When I run docker build, Docker reads the Dockerfile from top to bottom and follows each step one time. For every step, Docker makes a small saved change. These small saved changes are stacked together to form the image. The image is not a running application; it is just a stored copy of files and settings.
While building the image, Docker copies files from my computer into the image only when I use COPY. Docker also runs commands written with RUN, and whatever files those commands create are saved inside the image. All these files become part of the image forever unless the image is rebuilt. Docker also remembers some settings like environment values and the command that should be used to start the application later.
After the build is finished, the image contains the operating system files from the base image, the application files I copied, the files created during the build, and instructions about how the application should start. The Dockerfile itself is not used anymore.
Now I share this image with my friend. When my friend runs docker pull, Docker downloads the image into its own internal storage on his machine. The image is downloaded in pieces, and Docker puts those pieces together and unpacks them into files. These files are not placed in his project folder; they are kept inside Docker’s storage. At this point, the image is only stored, not running.
When my friend runs the image, Docker creates a container. A container is made by taking the image files and adding a small empty space on top where changes can happen. Docker then prepares the environment by setting the working directory, setting environment values, and choosing which user the program should run as.
After that, Docker starts exactly one program. This program is defined by ENTRYPOINT or CMD in the Dockerfile. Docker does not read the Dockerfile again, and it does not rebuild or download anything. It only starts the program.
The started program, which is usually a compiled binary, begins running like a normal Linux program. It reads environment values, reads data files that already exist inside the image, opens a network port, and keeps running. The program does not know it is inside Docker; it just sees files and folders.
All the files that the program reads must already be inside the image. If a required file was not copied during the build, the program will fail when the container runs. Any file changes made while the container is running are temporary and are removed when the container stops.
Inside Docker, image files are stored in layers. Each build step creates a layer that contains file changes. These layers are saved in a packed form when shared and are unpacked when downloaded. When the container runs, Docker joins all the layers together so the program sees one normal file system.
By default, Docker runs the program as the root user, but this is unsafe and hides problems. Running the program as a normal user is safer and forces correct file access. If the program fails as a normal user, it means the program depends on root power, which should be fixed.
In the end, Docker’s job is simple. It builds an image by collecting files and settings once, stores that image, and when asked to run it, starts one program using those stored files. Everything the program needs must already be inside the image before it starts.
________________________________________
One final line to remember
Docker builds files first, stores them as an image, and later runs one program that uses those stored files.
