Docker + Application Runtime — Master Notes
1️⃣ Build time vs Runtime (non-negotiable)
Build time
•	Happens during docker build
•	Instructions involved:
o	FROM, WORKDIR, COPY, RUN
•	Purpose:
o	Compile code
o	Prepare filesystem
o	Create an image
•	Happens once per image build
Runtime
•	Happens during docker run
•	Instructions involved:
o	ENTRYPOINT, CMD, ENV, USER
•	Purpose:
o	Start one Linux process
•	Happens every time container starts
Dockerfile is not executed at runtime
________________________________________
2️⃣ ENTRYPOINT vs CMD (exact meaning)
•	ENTRYPOINT → what process the container IS
•	CMD → default arguments to that process
Low level:
execve(ENTRYPOINT + CMD)
Best practice for services:
•	ENTRYPOINT = binary
•	CMD = optional args
________________________________________
3️⃣ Binary ≠ Application
Binary
•	Compiled Go code
•	Contains logic only
•	Produced by go build
Runtime data (NOT in binary)
•	JSON / YAML configs
•	Certs / keys
•	Templates
•	Migrations
•	Any file opened at runtime
If code uses os.Open / ReadFile → you must COPY it
________________________________________
4️⃣ Why COPY go.mod go.sum separately
Purpose: Docker layer caching
Pattern:
COPY go.mod go.sum ./
RUN go mod download
COPY . .
Why:
•	Dependencies change less often than code
•	Code change ≠ dependency re-download
•	Faster builds
Yes, COPY . . would work
No, it’s not optimal
________________________________________
5️⃣ What COPY . . actually does
•	Copies entire project from host
•	Into image filesystem
•	This is how source code enters container
•	go.mod/go.sum are copied again (harmless overwrite)
________________________________________
6️⃣ What go build really does
•	Compiles source code present in container filesystem
•	Links dependencies from /go/pkg/mod
•	Produces one binary
•	Does NOT run the app
•	Does NOT include external files
________________________________________
7️⃣ Multi-stage build (why it exists)
Builder stage
•	Has compilers & tools
•	Produces binary
Runtime stage
•	Clean OS image
•	Contains only:
o	binary
o	runtime data
•	Smaller, safer, faster
Build tools are never needed at runtime
________________________________________
8️⃣ Runtime data — what to look for in code
Runtime data = anything touched after binary starts
Look for:
•	os.Open, ReadFile → files
•	os.Getenv → env vars
•	os.Create, writes → writable paths
•	TLS / cert loading
•	DB connections
If you miss runtime data → container crashes in prod
________________________________________
9️⃣ Ports & ENV (common confusion)
•	App binds to a port (developer decision)
•	ENV provides config
•	Docker/Kubernetes route traffic
ENV does NOT:
•	expose ports
•	open firewall
•	publish networking
________________________________________
🔟 ENTRYPOINT does NOT run Dockerfile
ENTRYPOINT:
•	Starts the binary
•	Binary becomes PID 1
•	Dockerfile steps are already done
If binary exits → container stops
________________________________________
1️⃣1️⃣ Root vs non-root (critical)
Default
•	Containers run as root
•	Root hides bugs
•	Security risk
Non-root
•	Exposes bad assumptions
•	Forces correct permissions
•	Required in Kubernetes prod
Rule:
If app only works as root → app is broken
________________________________________
1️⃣2️⃣ Permission issues (truth)
Yes, running as user can cause permission errors.
That’s GOOD.
It tells you:
•	where app writes files
•	what directories must exist
•	what ownership is required
Fix at build time:
•	create directories
•	chown them
•	then switch user
Never:
•	chmod 777
•	“just run as root”
________________________________________
1️⃣3️⃣ Dockerfile is DERIVED, not invented
Before writing Dockerfile, you must know:
1.	What command starts the app?
2.	What files are read at runtime?
3.	What env vars are required?
4.	What port app binds to?
5.	What user should run it?
6.	What tools are build-only?
If you can answer these → Dockerfile is easy.
________________________________________
Final mental lock 🔒
•	Docker runs processes, not code
•	Binary = logic, data = separate
•	Build time ≠ runtime
•	ENTRYPOINT defines purpose
•	Non-root exposes reality
•	Dockerfile mirrors application behavior
